.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm libeventlog
library lets a user-space process emit events into the kernel
.Xr eventlog 9
framework using the same schema, generated header, and
.Dv _LOG
macros that in-kernel producers use.
A schema-generated
.Aq Ar provider Ns _eventlog.h ,
when compiled without
.Dv _KERNEL ,
pulls in
.In sys/eventlog.h
to obtain the
.Vt "struct eventlog_session"
handle and the
.Fn eventlog_event_write
family that the macros expand to.
The generated macro is identical in both environments; only the linked
runtime differs.
.Ss Scaling model
The library is built for a process that handles a very large number of
connections, where
.Em a connection is a session .
Two object kinds, with very different costs, make this affordable:
.Bl -tag -width ".Sy Session" -offset indent
.It Sy Provider
A logical event domain, registered once with
.Fn eventlog_provider_create .
A provider is the
.Em only
thing that consumes a
.Pa /dev/eventlog
descriptor, a kernel registration, and an enablement page.
A process registers a small, fixed set of providers
.Pq typically one ;
do not register a provider per connection.
.It Sy Session
A cheap per-connection handle created with
.Fn eventlog_session_create .
A session owns no descriptor, no mapping, and no buffer: it is just a
.Fa session_id
stamped into every record plus a pointer to the effective enablement word.
A process may hold hundreds of thousands of live sessions.
.El
.Pp
All sessions and providers in the process share
.Em one
descriptor.
By default they also share
.Em one
submission ring.
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_PER_THREAD_RINGS
on the first
.Fn eventlog_provider_create
in the process instead gives each writing thread its own SPSC ring
.Pq 64 KB by default .
Each record self-describes its
.Fa provider_id
and
.Fa session_id ,
so the kernel drain demultiplexes per record; there is no
per-session or per-provider buffer to size or drain.
The kernel auto-manages its receive-side stream buffers, so a subscriber
declares nothing
.Pq see Xr elog 5 .
.Pp
.Fn eventlog_provider_create
registers
.Fa name
on the process's shared
.Pa /dev/eventlog
channel
.Pq opening it on first use
and returns an opaque provider handle.
Events are emitted through a session derived from the provider with
.Fn eventlog_session_create ,
mirroring the kernel two-step in
.Xr eventlog 9 .
The
.Fa config
argument may be
.Dv NULL
to select defaults; otherwise it points at a
.Vt "struct eventlog_provider_config" :
.Bl -tag -width ".Va subscribers_changed" -offset indent
.It Va default_enabled
Seed value for the
.Va kern.eventlog. Ns Aq Ar name Ns .default
sysctl.
.It Va flags
Bitmask of
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_*
bits.
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_AUTO_RATIO
opts the name into
.Va kern.eventlog. Ns Aq Ar name Ns .auto_ratio .
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_EXTERNAL_CONTROL
skips the private control thread;
the caller must invoke
.Fn eventlog_provider_poll
about every 100ms
so dump, subscriber, and default callbacks still run.
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_PER_THREAD_RINGS
gives each writing thread its own SPSC submission ring
instead of the process-wide multi-writer ring.
The first provider registered in the process sets this; later providers
on the same channel inherit it.
This requires a kernel with multi-ring support
.Pq ring header Dv EVENTLOG_RING_VERSION No >= 3 ,
which the kernel advertises in the read-only
.Va kern.eventlog.user_rings_max
sysctl
.Pq absent on older kernels .
On an older kernel the first thread's
.Dv EVENTLOG_IOCTL_SETUP_RING
fails with
.Er EEXIST
and the whole channel falls back to the shared multi-writer ring, so no
thread's writes are lost.
.Fn eventlog_user_per_thread_rings_supported
reports whether the running kernel supports per-thread rings; a producer
that statically links libeventlog and may run on an older kernel should
call it once at startup and set
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_PER_THREAD_RINGS
only when it returns non-zero.
.It Va dump_callback , Va dump_callback_arg
Optional callback invoked when a subscriber requests a state replay
.Pq the Dv DUMP_STATE subscription flag .
Emit the provider's current state with the ordinary
.Fn eventlog_event_write
family; the library scopes those events to the requesting subscriber.
.It Va default_changed , Va default_changed_arg
Optional callback invoked when the provider's
.Va default
tunable changes
.Pq the raw sysctl value is passed through .
.It Va subscribers_changed , Va subscribers_changed_arg
Optional callback invoked when the provider crosses the
zero-to-some/some-to-zero subscriber boundary
.Pq Fa has_subscribers No true or false ,
useful for gating expensive bookkeeping when nobody is listening.
.El
.Pp
The callbacks have the same signatures as the in-kernel provider callbacks
in
.Xr eventlog 9 :
each receives the
.Vt "struct eventlog_provider *"
handle and its registered
.Va Ns _arg .
By default they run on a private per-process background thread.
With
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_EXTERNAL_CONTROL
they run on the thread that calls
.Fn eventlog_provider_poll .
They are optional, and the hot paths
.Pq the Dv _ENABLED No gate and event writes
never wait on that dispatch.
.Pp
.Fn eventlog_provider_create
also
.Xr mmap 2 Ns s
the provider's kernel-maintained enablement page out of the shared channel
mapping.
The kernel keeps the leading words of that page
.Pq Va effective_level , Va effective_keywords
up to date with the aggregated interest of all current subscribers.
A session reads those words through an internal
.Va eff
pointer, which the generated
.Dv _ENABLED
macro dereferences.
When no subscriber is interested the macro neither packs the payload nor
enters the kernel, so a disabled event costs only the indirection plus a
pair of loads.
If the running kernel predates mmap support the library transparently
falls back to a heap-backed, always-enabled provider; per-subscriber
filtering still occurs in the kernel on push.
.Ss Per-connection sessions
.Fn eventlog_session_create
creates a cheap session on
.Fa provider ,
stamping
.Fa session_id
into every record it emits so a subscriber can group one connection's
events.
The
.Fa waitok ,
.Fa create_payload ,
and
.Fa create_payload_size
arguments mirror the kernel
.Xr eventlog 9
signature and are presently ignored by the user runtime
.Pq it always heap-allocates and emits no SESSION_CREATE event .
It performs no system call and allocates only the small handle, so it is
suitable on a per-connection basis.
By default a new session inherits the provider's current enablement.
The
.Va kern.eventlog. Ns Aq Ar name Ns .auto_ratio
sysctl does not apply to user providers: the kernel never sees user-space
session creates, so a producer that wants 1-in-N sampling selects sessions
itself and drives
.Fn eventlog_session_set_enabled .
.Fn eventlog_session_set_enabled
with
.Fa enabled
zero silences a session regardless of subscriber interest;
.Fa enabled
non-zero returns it to inheriting the provider's kernel-published state.
.Fn eventlog_session_set_filter
pins an explicit
.Fa level
and
.Fa keywords
on the session, ignoring provider state.
All three are O(1) pointer repoints with no system call and no fan-out, so
they are cheap to call as a connection's logging policy changes.
.Fn eventlog_session_destroy
releases a session, freeing the small handle.
.Pp
.Fn eventlog_event_write
writes one event whose scalar payload is
.Fa length
bytes at
.Fa buffer
into the channel's submission ring.
.Fn eventlog_event_write_gather
is the variable-length form, gathering
.Fa iovcnt
.Fa iov
segments into a single payload.
The
.Fa id ,
.Fa level ,
and
.Fa keywords
arguments are supplied by the generated macro and drive per-subscriber
filtering in the kernel.
The
.Fn ...\&_at
variants take an explicit
.Fa timestamp_us
.Pq microseconds on Dv CLOCK_UPTIME ;
passing zero, as the plain variants do, timestamps the event at the moment
it is written.
Timestamps are forced strictly increasing across the shared ring so the
kernel can merge user events into subscriber buffers without reordering.
.Pp
Events from every session and provider on a channel are written into the
.Em one
shared, memory-mapped submission ring and published with a release store;
no system call is issued on the steady-state write path.
The kernel drains the ring lazily from the subscriber
.Xr read 2
path and a periodic poll, re-validating and fanning every record out to all
current subscribers, so a consumer of this API never has to manage delivery
or flush anything: each write publishes the ring head with a release store,
and the library exposes no client-visible buffering to drain.
The library issues an occasional drain
.Pq a single Xr ioctl 2
only when the kernel publishes a hint in the ring header that one is
worthwhile: a subscriber is blocked waiting for data, or the ring has crossed
a high-water mark while a subscriber is attached.
This bounds delivery latency and ring drops without a system call per event.
.Fn eventlog_provider_destroy
unregisters a provider.
Destroying the last provider in a process publishes any pending writes,
unmaps the ring, and closes the shared channel.
The provider and channel are freed once every session derived from the
provider has also been destroyed, so destroy order does not matter.
.Pp
The write functions return
.Vt void
and silently drop events on error, matching the kernel producer's
fire-and-forget semantics; payloads larger than
.Dv UINT16_MAX
bytes are dropped.
If the submission ring is full
.Pq the kernel has not drained it fast enough ,
further writes are dropped and counted in the ring's
.Va dropped
header field rather than blocking or overwriting unconsumed records.
.Ss Concurrency and fork
A session may be used for concurrent
.Dv _LOG
from many threads.
By default, writers reserve space in the shared buffer with a lock-free atomic
compare-and-swap, copy their payload in parallel, and commit in reservation
order, so parallel producers neither lose nor corrupt records and never
serialize on a lock.
A writer whose predecessor has not yet published the ring's head
spins, yielding the CPU, until that commit lands; the waiter keeps its
reservation for the whole wait so a preempted predecessor still unblocks
later
.Dv _LOG
calls when it commits.
If a predecessor is dead in the copy window
.Pq ptrace-stopped, or cancelled mid-copy
later writers on that channel wait until the process exits.
With
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_PER_THREAD_RINGS
each writing thread publishes into its own ring with a plain store of
.Va head ;
events from different threads are distinct streams that the kernel merge
orders by timestamp, and per-thread emission order is preserved.
Distinct sessions are independent and need no external locking.
.Pp
The library runs one background thread per process
.Pq created on the first Fn eventlog_provider_create , No joined on the last destroy
that owns the kernel control channel, unless a provider is created with
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_EXTERNAL_CONTROL .
In that mode the caller must invoke
.Fn eventlog_provider_poll
about every 100ms;
that call dispatches the same callbacks on the calling thread and reports
session counts about once a second.
Concurrent
.Fn eventlog_provider_poll
calls on the same process are serialized.
.Xr fork 2
is not supported in the child without re-opening: that thread does not
survive the fork, and a
.Xr pthread_atfork 3
child handler marks the inherited submission ring unusable so the child's
writes drop rather than racing the parent's in-flight state.
A child that needs to produce events must call
.Fn eventlog_provider_create
afresh, which builds a fresh channel and, unless using
.Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_EXTERNAL_CONTROL ,
a new control thread.
.Sh BUILD INTEGRATION
A client gets the in-kernel workflow with one line.
Add to the program or library
.Xr make 1
file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
EVENTLOG_SCHEMA=	myprov_eventlog_schema.src
.Ed
.Pp
This generates
.Aq Ar myprov Ns _eventlog.h
from the schema, adds the include path, and links
.Nm libeventlog
automatically
.Pq see Pa share/mk/bsd.eventlog.mk .
The source then only needs to
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <eventlog/myprov_eventlog.h>
.Ed
.Pp
and call the generated
.No MYPROV_EVENTLOG_ Ns Aq Ar EVENT Ns _LOG
macro.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn eventlog_provider_create
returns a provider handle and
.Fn eventlog_session_create
returns a session handle, or
.Dv NULL
with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Fn eventlog_provider_poll
returns the number of control events dispatched, or
.Li -1
with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn eventlog_provider_create
may fail with any error returned by
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
or
.Xr mmap 2 ,
and in addition:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa name
is
.Dv NULL ,
empty, or too long,
or a provider in this process already registered with a different control model
.Pq every provider must agree on Dv EVENTLOG_PROVIDER_EXTERNAL_CONTROL .
.El
.Pp
.Fn eventlog_provider_poll
may fail with any error returned by
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
and in addition:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa provider
is
.Dv NULL .
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The library's control thread is already running.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Given
.Pa myprov_eventlog_schema.src
defining a provider
.Dq myprov
with a scalar event
.Dq startup :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <eventlog/myprov_eventlog.h>

struct eventlog_provider *prov;
struct eventlog_session *conn;

/* Once per process: register the provider. */
prov = eventlog_provider_create("myprov", NULL);
if (prov == NULL)
	err(1, "eventlog_provider_create");

/* Per connection: a cheap session keyed by the connection id. */
conn = eventlog_session_create(prov, connection_id, false, NULL, 0);
if (conn == NULL)
	err(1, "eventlog_session_create");

struct myprov_startup ev = { .pid = getpid() };
MYPROV_EVENTLOG_STARTUP_LOG(conn, &ev);

eventlog_session_destroy(conn);	/* when the connection ends */
eventlog_provider_destroy(prov);	/* at process shutdown */
.Ed
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Producing events does
.Em not
require privilege.
.Pa /dev/eventlog
is world readable and writable, and the kernel validates and bounce-copies
every record drained from a provider's submission ring, so an unprivileged
process
.Pq for example an
.Xr nginx 8
worker running as
.Dq www
may register a provider and emit events without running as root.
.Pp
Reading the event stream is privileged: a subscriber observes every
provider's events system-wide, so the
.Dv EVENTLOG_IOCTL_CREATE
subscribe path is restricted to the super-user and members of the
.Dq operator
group
.Pq see Xr elog 1 .
A producer's wired submission-ring memory is charged against its real user id
and capped by the
.Va kern.eventlog.user_ring_max_bytes_per_uid
sysctl, bounding the kernel memory an untrusted producer can pin.
.Sh BUILDING
In-tree programs set
.Va EVENTLOG_SCHEMA
in the Makefile;
.Pa share/mk/bsd.eventlog.mk
generates the producer header and links this library.
Ports set
.Va EVENTLOG_SCHEMA
and include
.Pa ports/Mk/bsd.eventlog.port.mk
for the same header generation.
The library installs
.Pa libeventlog.pc
for pkg-config.
The schema compiler
.Pa eventlog_gen.awk
is installed in
.Pa /usr/share/eventlog
and advertised as the
.Va eventlog_gen
pkg-config variable.
